@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.51}Exercise 4.51:} Implement a new kind of
assignment called @code{permanentAssign} that is not undone upon failure.  For
example, we can choose two distinct elements from a list and count the number
of trials required to make a successful choice as follows:

@example typescript
const transcript = ambDriverLoop(
  makeAmbEvaluator(@{ permanentSet: true @}),
  env,
  [
  "let count = 0;",
  `
  const x = anElementOf(["a", "b", "c"]);
  const y = anElementOf(["a", "b", "c"]);
  permanentAssign(count, count + 1);
  require(x !== y);
  [x, y, count];
  `,
]);
// ;;; Amb-Eval input:
// ;;; Starting a new problem
// ;;; Amb-Eval value:
// ["a", "b", 2]
//
// ;;; Amb-Eval input:
// try-again
// ;;; Amb-Eval value:
// ["a", "c", 3]
@end example

What values would have been displayed if we had used ordinary assignment here
rather than @code{permanentAssign}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.52}Exercise 4.52:} Implement a new construct called
@code{ifFail} that permits the user to catch the failure of an expression.
@code{IfFail} takes two expressions.  It evaluates the first expression as
usual and returns as usual if the evaluation succeeds.  If the evaluation
fails, however, the value of the second expression is returned, as in the
following example:

@example typescript
const transcript = ambDriverLoop(
  makeAmbEvaluator(@{ ifFail: true @}),
  env,
  [
  `ifFail(
    (() => @{
      const x = anElementOf([1, 3, 5]);
      require(x % 2 === 0);
      return x;
    @})(),
    "all-odd",
  )`,
  `ifFail(
    (() => @{
      const x = anElementOf([1, 3, 5, 8]);
      require(x % 2 === 0);
      return x;
    @})(),
    "all-odd",
  )`,
]);

// ;;; Amb-Eval input:
// ;;; Starting a new problem
// ;;; Amb-Eval value:
// all-odd
//
// ;;; Amb-Eval input:
// ;;; Starting a new problem
// ;;; Amb-Eval value:
// 8
@end example
@end quotation

